n't respond to their  antidepressant on the first time  trials  uh with pharmacogenomics testing not  only are we cutting back those months  for  um of getting to be mentally stable  position  and increasing adherence there's also 52  decrease in hospital readmission 42  reduction er visit and 85 percent  decrease in mortality which is huge  and also saves a lot of health care  costs as well  pgx is a tool or genomics for short  pgx for short is a tool that we can use  to guide therapy  just like blood pressure guidelines or  diabetic diabetes guidelines  so here are some key enzyme players in  mental health this is not a complete  list  but just a selected few sip 2d6 is a big  player but there are other genes that  play a role in mental health  and have pgx indications such as zip2c19  3a4 sl6 and  slc6a4 i'm not going to go through all  of them today but wanted to touch on  sip2d6 mainly for today  and sl6 slc-6a4  is a key transporter that's targeted um  there's a target of  a lot of ssris and it's said that people  with short alleles are more prone to  side effects and  delayed responses ssri  so just to put in perspective and kind  of give a visual  uh this is a pathway of ssris but it's a  specifically um shows prozac i know  that's a little busy  slide but if if you see the asterisks on  there it represents the main key enzyme  player for ssri such as prozac  so on the left you'll see they are  enantiomer with a small portion that's  unchanged and cleared out of the body  but on the right you'll see the  f enantiomer which is mainly metabolized  by 2d6  to the more potent metabolite of the s  enantiomer  it's almost 20 times more potent other  enzymes listed also play a part like  the 3a4 to c19 and 3a5 but only  they only become important once you have  chronic dosing  when the contribution of the 2d6 is  diminished by  inhibition through fluoxetine or nor  fluoxetine  so if the 2d6 enzyme is not active or a  patient is a poor metabolizer  the the conversion does not take place  and we have prozac just accumulating on  the left  side also prozac inhibits its own  metabolism which is why it has a long  half-life  so you can imagine in the case of the  little boy he must have been a poor  metabolizer  and accumulation must have been really  huge  so 25 of all drugs again are metabolized  by this enzyme 62d6 and and being a poor  metabolizer is more common than we think  45 of asians are poor metabolizer  it's a huge percentage so i also have  the ultralapid metabolic  percentage of for the completion on here  which is on the other  side of the spectrum that also needs to  be looked at but it's not  enough to just look at the enzyme  activity of just one medication we also  need to look at drug gene interaction as  well  so this is where phenoconversion comes  into play so  the definition is that there is a  mismatch between an  individual genotype-based prediction of  a gene metabolism  metabolism and its through capacity so  in other words there is a mismatch  between  clinically observed phenotype and the  phenotype you expect to see from a  patient's genotype  there's a this is a great example of why  we don't want to interpret  a pgx report in isolation we want to  look at both drug drug interaction and  drug gene interaction  so you can be a normal metabolizer but  because you added an external  factor such as let's say smoking or even  another medication  you pheno convert to a poor metabolizer  let's say  and strong inhibitors such as prozac can  phenoconvert a patient to a poor  metabolizer  and the opposite is true also a strong  inducer can  turn in turn a person into ultra rapid  metabolizer  so we call this the blind spot of  pharmacogenomics or what is better known  as the achilles heel  so it's not enough again to look at just  one medication and see how person is  metabolizing it but  rather look at the drug gene interaction  as well again  the great example is sip2d6 and and i'm  going to use prozac to be consistent  throughout my presentation  prozac is a strong inhibitor of 2d6  and if we add tramadol let's say for  pain on there  which uses 2d6 to activate we have just  phenoconverted that individual  if the person was a normal metabolizer  of 2d6  but adding tramadol made them a poor  metabolizer of tramadol  so again 25 of all drugs are metabolized  by 2d6 so it's it's important which is  why this  um medicaid this drug is really well  studied  a lot of studies have been done about it  so here's an actual case study done on a  42 year old female who  came into her primary care with shoulder  pain and was prescribed  codeine with acetaminophen she did well  and her pain was  pretty much managed just fine so this  patient is a normal metabolizer of  codeine this  chart is going to show you the affinity  of a drug to an enzyme  so codeine was a weak has a weak  affinity substrate for 2d6 which is  indicated here as the 10  coding is also an inactive medication  needing the 2d6 to activate to morphine  to come to have the pain relief and we  also see acetaminophen metabolized by  step 1a2 but there's no interaction  there so we don't really worry about it  so months later there there was a mass  in her lungs that was found and she  needed some emotional  support so she started taking fluoxetine  10 milligrams  every day and she got increased to 20  milligram every day over the next  several weeks  her pain started to increase so her  coding dose was also increased it went  from 30 milligrams  four times a day to 60 milligram four  times a day  so now we see that fluoxetine is a  strong sip to dc  substrate uh 40 and extensively  metabolized by that same enzyme  and as we saw earlier it's also  metabolized by two  c9 but that's just five percent but  since  now there's another substrate with  stronger an affinity to  to see a 2d6 the metabolized  metabolism of coating is diminished this  is a great example of phenoconversion  the patient was a normal metabolizer of